Well, this is Clark Green.

32
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:25,840
Welcome back to the ScoutMaster Podcast.

33
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:31,120
Listen, comments and questions about the password joke you just heard, you can send those directly

34
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:35,560
to Larry Geiger because Larry is the one who sent in the joke.

35
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:36,880
Thanks Larry, I think.

36
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:40,840
Hey, the great storm came and went.

37
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:41,840
That's right.

38
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:43,580
Hurricane Irene came and went.

39
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:45,560
It left our community fairly untouched.

40
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:49,720
It was more of a glancing blow than a big fat punch in the face.

41
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:53,080
and that being said I know there's a lot of communities up and down the eastern

42
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:55,720
seaboard who suffered a great deal more than us

43
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:59,800
and my heartfelt encouragement if you've been handed travel over the past few

44
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:02,600
days things will get better. Walter Underwood is a new

45
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:07,800
contributor at scoutmastercg.com. Walter found the blog

46
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:10,440
when I started writing it about six years ago

47
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:15,079
and he has been a faithful commenter and he's participated in a lot of

48
00:02:15,079 --> 00:02:19,079
conversations on the blog. Walter and I have tried have

49
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:21,640
played tag with one another trying to set up a

50
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:26,120
podcast interview and maybe maybe one of these days we'll get around to it

51
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:30,520
but I invited Walter to become a occasional author

52
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:34,520
and contributor to the blog and he was kind enough to consent to do that and

53
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:37,960
to offer his experience as a scout leader and a scout master

54
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:44,280
and as a young man he was a scout go on the blog at scoutmastercg.com

55
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:49,320
and look in the about column and you'll find Walter's

56
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:53,240
profile there with a little bit about Walter

57
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:56,840
and then do look at the first article that we posted this past week

58
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:00,920
it was about how the adult leaders in his troops sat down and they had a

59
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:05,160
brief but meaningful discussion about how well they were applying the eight

60
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:08,280
methods of scouting. I thought it was a really great idea

61
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:12,520
and I will be repeating such a discussion in our troop very soon

62
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:17,640
and thanks Walter thanks for doing that and thanks to all of you who've been in

63
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:20,680
touch you know we were away for a while there in

64
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:25,320
August for about three or four weeks and now vacation season is drawing to a

65
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:27,480
close and school's getting ready to start up

66
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:32,040
and scouting will begin with that full slate of fall activities that we all

67
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:35,800
have scheduled and I look forward to hearing what you're up to.

68
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:39,560
This time around on the podcast we're going to answer two email questions

69
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:43,160
that have come in over the past week. One kind of

70
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:46,280
expands on our discussion of discipline and scouting

71
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:49,720
last week and it's about how youth leadership can get

72
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:54,520
scouts to listen to them that's a familiar problem

73
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:58,200
a pretty perennial problem in scouts and then there was another

74
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:02,040
question about scoutmaster conferences and a little bit different

75
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:05,560
slant on scoutmaster conferences this time so that's what we're going to be

76
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:08,440
doing in this podcast I think that's plenty don't you?

77
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,560
Yeah sure why not Clark let's get started shall we?

78
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:17,560
Find me a letter send it my name

79
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:22,040
email that is folks and here's an answer to one of your

80
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:24,920
emails

81
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:35,240
Scoutmaster Michael Beck wrote in and he said a couple of my scouts have come

82
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:37,320
to me struggling with the question of how to

83
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:41,800
get their scouts to listen to them I'd like to discuss this and give advice to

84
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:45,000
them at the troop leader training we have planned this weekend

85
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,800
tell me prepare could you give me your thoughts and tips

86
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:52,840
well I'm more than happy to Mike and and thanks for being in touch

87
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:56,600
here's what I've you know this is what my experience tells me scouts will

88
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:00,200
listen to the directions of their peers most of the time

89
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:05,000
as long as those directions are fair as long as they're reasonably

90
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:09,640
well-reasoned as long as they appeal to the personal wants and needs of the

91
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:15,320
scouts who's being directed and are made in a very respectful way

92
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:18,840
and that means as a request rather than an order

93
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:22,680
now scouts aren't listening to their peers one of those aspects

94
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:26,520
is most likely missing or there's just some kind of basic

95
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:29,800
perceptual difference of opinion or there's

96
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:34,600
some other underlying reason why they need to be disobedient or defiant

97
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:38,840
if a youth leader makes a request it's not obeyed

98
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:44,600
I think the best thing for him to do is just to go down that little list

99
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:48,360
and see if he included all the elements above now it sounds like a long drawn

100
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:50,760
out process but only take a couple of seconds once they've

101
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:53,880
practiced it a little bit and then they need to go back to the

102
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:57,160
scout and see if they can modify the request

103
00:05:57,160 --> 00:06:01,480
if the youth leader has included all those elements

104
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:05,400
you know fair well-reasoned appeals to the scouts personal wants and needs

105
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:10,920
and it's made in a respectful way we need to see if there's some kind of a

106
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:15,400
perceptual difference about what's being asked or if it's just being defiant

107
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:19,960
and you know the patrol leader just needs to try and reason with the guy

108
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:23,480
listen did you understand what I what I asked you and why I asked it I mean

109
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:27,000
I'm not trying to be the big boss here but I am the patrol leader we do need

110
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,360
to get things done maybe I can explain why

111
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:34,520
better is there something you you know would you rather take one of the other

112
00:06:34,520 --> 00:06:37,160
jobs well do you understand we all have to share

113
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:41,080
in doing this and that's you know that's pretty mature thing for

114
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:45,000
a a young patrol leader to do I understand

115
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,280
giving them as many tools to work with as possible

116
00:06:48,280 --> 00:06:53,000
seems to help here's one of the things I tell my youth leadership during our

117
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,880
troop leader training do you know what happened

118
00:06:55,880 --> 00:07:01,159
August 1st of 1907 that's right you trivia hounds you got it

119
00:07:01,159 --> 00:07:05,800
that is the day that Baden Powell the founder of Scouting

120
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:12,520
took a group of boys to Brown Sea Island for the first scouting camp out ever

121
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:18,840
now they elected patrol leaders that day right around noontime just about

122
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:23,000
noontime and by 1201 that patrol leader had

123
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,360
given his first direction to the scouts in his patrol

124
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:31,320
right around 1202 one of those scouts either ignored him

125
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:34,760
and ran off somewhere else to do something or he was just outwardly

126
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:39,000
defiant and said no I'm not going to do that and that began

127
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,120
a great tradition of scouting that's right ignoring your patrol leader or

128
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:46,920
telling your patrol leader no I'm not going to do that

129
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:50,200
ever since then every patrol leader's ever been elected

130
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:55,479
every single one has been ignored and or disrespected to some extent

131
00:07:55,479 --> 00:07:59,719
so when we experience this in our patrols and in our troop we can remember

132
00:07:59,719 --> 00:08:06,520
that you know it's a grand tradition a venerable tradition in scouting

133
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:10,440
and you need not be upset by it you need to expect it

134
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:14,599
and it's going to happen so what you need to know

135
00:08:14,599 --> 00:08:18,760
is how you're going to handle this when it does happen

136
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:23,159
and every leadership position in scouting is one of service to the scouts

137
00:08:23,159 --> 00:08:26,200
you didn't become a patrol leader or senior patrol leader or have another

138
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:30,120
leadership position because we thought you made a great

139
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:33,000
boss and you should be able to boss everybody around

140
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:37,000
no you you became that leader because we think

141
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:41,400
and your fellow scouts think that you are best suited to serve

142
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:47,160
in that position so serving them means that you need to think

143
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:50,920
about making requests so the people you are serving

144
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:55,000
rather than issuing orders to them you're not a drill sergeant

145
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,880
you know you're not the president of the united states

146
00:08:57,880 --> 00:09:01,319
you're not the captain of the football team you need to

147
00:09:01,319 --> 00:09:04,439
see this as service that you're doing for others

148
00:09:04,439 --> 00:09:08,439
and you need to get them involved and encourage them to cooperate

149
00:09:08,439 --> 00:09:12,280
so you need to be fair and relevant and patient and respectful

150
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:15,880
and you'll get good results most of the time now if a scout in your

151
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:19,240
patrol is continuously disruptive or just

152
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:22,840
absolutely disobedient then you need to call on the senior patrol leader

153
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:26,439
that's the next guy and he's going to come in and try to help you

154
00:09:26,439 --> 00:09:30,920
if he's unsuccessful guess who's next that's me

155
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:34,439
so that scout who's causing a problem is going to get

156
00:09:34,439 --> 00:09:37,320
referred to me and we're going to sit down and have a discussion about

157
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:40,760
whatever's bothering him now sitting down to have a discussion

158
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:44,280
with me at least in our troop is something that boys do not look

159
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:48,839
forward to not in those circumstances okay

160
00:09:48,839 --> 00:09:52,839
if if you've been disobedient or you've been a problem

161
00:09:52,839 --> 00:09:55,880
and you're going to have to come and talk to me if it gets to that point which

162
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:59,160
is pretty rare then they know that that's not

163
00:09:59,160 --> 00:10:04,600
going to be a fun time so this is what i tell every scout

164
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:09,319
at the earliest scout master conferences and i reinforce it

165
00:10:09,319 --> 00:10:13,640
uh several times over but this is what i tell them i said now you won't often

166
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:16,840
hear me or any of the other adults telling you to do something in Boy Scouts

167
00:10:16,840 --> 00:10:20,280
because that's not the way Boy Scouts works adults don't end up telling you

168
00:10:20,280 --> 00:10:23,160
what to do your patrol leader and your senior

169
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:27,000
patrol leader run the program and they'll be asking you

170
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,640
to do what needs to be done sometimes they'll be giving you directions

171
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:35,080
and and i want you to follow those directions because they're for your own

172
00:10:35,080 --> 00:10:38,200
good and it's the way scouting works

173
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:41,080
you're not going to do this you're going to do everything that you're told

174
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:45,480
because you are mature and you understand that it's for your own good

175
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:49,000
but sometimes scouts decide they don't need to listen to their youth leaders

176
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:53,480
now if that happens and they can't persuade you that uh to get on the team

177
00:10:53,480 --> 00:10:56,840
and to cooperate you're going to come and talk to me for a while

178
00:10:56,840 --> 00:10:59,400
and if you have to come and talk to me for a while that means you're going to

179
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:03,880
miss out on whatever the fun is that everyone else is having at the time

180
00:11:03,880 --> 00:11:08,120
if you ask the other scouts who've had to sit and talk to me about things

181
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:12,280
like this and ain't all that much fun so that's what i that's what i tell them

182
00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:16,280
at the scout masters conference and naturally my older scouts kind of play

183
00:11:16,280 --> 00:11:19,560
up what a horrible thing it is to have to be referred to the scout master

184
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:24,760
it's it's dishonorable if you have to be referred to the scout master

185
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:29,640
you're just beyond the pale and if you have to be referred to the scout master

186
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:31,880
it's kind of the worst thing it could ever happen to you because now you're

187
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:33,960
going to have to sit down and talk to him

188
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:39,720
now i'm not really mean i'm not and i'm not but i'm comfortable

189
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:44,840
with being kind of the boogeyman about this because it works

190
00:11:44,840 --> 00:11:49,400
in the rare instance a scout does end up being referred to me

191
00:11:49,400 --> 00:11:55,880
well he comes over and he sits down and i glower at him a little bit

192
00:11:55,880 --> 00:12:00,040
and i ask him somewhat sharply what he's been up to and he mumbles a bit

193
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:03,480
and you know looks at his shoes then i remind him

194
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:06,920
of the discussion we had at his scout masters conference

195
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:11,240
and i ask if he's been treated badly or unfairly because you know patrol leaders

196
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:15,560
make mistakes and senior patrol leaders make mistakes we expect them to right

197
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:20,280
but almost always the answer is no he hasn't really been treated unfairly

198
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:23,959
and then you know we we talk about the problem a little bit

199
00:12:23,959 --> 00:12:28,520
a very very little bit and then he sits there for five minutes or 10 minutes

200
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:33,400
absolute complete silence now i don't call it a timeout because that makes the boy

201
00:12:33,400 --> 00:12:37,079
feel like a toddler and i don't want to demean him

202
00:12:37,079 --> 00:12:41,240
but that's exactly what i'm doing it's a timeout and timeouts are useful

203
00:12:41,240 --> 00:12:45,800
because they make you sit and think and so the scout gets to sit and think for a

204
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:48,760
while and when i think that he's had enough time

205
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:54,520
i send him back to his patrol and i say you know just listen and do what you're

206
00:12:54,520 --> 00:12:58,360
asked to do and then i also tell him that by no means

207
00:12:58,360 --> 00:13:01,720
does he want to have to come back and talk to me again about this

208
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:05,880
they usually get the message go back and they're reasonably cooperative

209
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:09,160
now this doesn't always work it's not it's there's no silver bullet for this

210
00:13:09,160 --> 00:13:13,240
of course you know youth leaders and adult leaders too we take

211
00:13:13,240 --> 00:13:17,640
disobedience or or being ignored as a pretty personal upfront

212
00:13:17,640 --> 00:13:22,040
and we can get pretty touchy about it i mean it can it can really make you

213
00:13:22,040 --> 00:13:25,719
angry it can make you frustrated it can make you depressed

214
00:13:25,719 --> 00:13:31,319
you know it's a pretty common feeling it takes a lot of patience and time to

215
00:13:31,319 --> 00:13:35,719
earn the respect of your peers no matter how old you are

216
00:13:35,719 --> 00:13:39,640
you know that from the work that you do outside of scouting

217
00:13:39,640 --> 00:13:43,640
most likely for that new patrol leader it's the first time they've ever

218
00:13:43,640 --> 00:13:47,240
encountered the situation of of having to be a leader

219
00:13:47,240 --> 00:13:51,400
and having to ask people to cooperate and to do things and when people don't

220
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:54,920
do that they take it pretty personally and it's pretty painful it can be pretty

221
00:13:54,920 --> 00:13:59,240
emotional it can make a guy once he's had that

222
00:13:59,240 --> 00:14:03,319
happen you make him want to quit and not not be a leader anymore

223
00:14:03,319 --> 00:14:07,079
so you kind of have to work and empathize with them and and you know

224
00:14:07,079 --> 00:14:11,240
let them know that it's a price of doing business as a leader no matter what age

225
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:15,079
no matter what situation or no matter what's your station in life

226
00:14:15,079 --> 00:14:18,680
you're going to run into these problems the way

227
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:22,600
adults relate to patrol leaders and other youth leadership in the troop

228
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:28,040
is also crucially important to make sure that things are running smoothly i mean

229
00:14:28,040 --> 00:14:31,000
youth leaders are expected to make mistakes and misjudged situations

230
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:34,680
that's a given and we have the provisional authority to step in and to

231
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:39,560
stop any activity that we see as being inappropriate or unsafe

232
00:14:39,560 --> 00:14:45,000
but how we react when they make misjudgments or make mistakes

233
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,360
is incredibly important to their being able to command respect from their

234
00:14:48,360 --> 00:14:53,480
fellow scouts we should never ever countermand

235
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:57,400
directions given by a youth leader in front of other scouts

236
00:14:57,400 --> 00:15:01,480
if it's at all possible like i said in in times where something is unsafe or

237
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:06,440
inappropriate you know is you might have to do that

238
00:15:06,440 --> 00:15:10,040
